d.line("- `Reserver` (Protocol, in `llm/callgate.py`)", """- Exact signatures (readiness major 3). `CallGate`, `Reserver` and `build_gate` live in `llm/callgate.py`; `GateSettings` lives in `llm/spend.py` so `config` is not imported (8.1):

```python
class GateSettings(Protocol):  # the two spend numbers the gate reads
    max_plausible_tokens: int
    reserve_margin: Decimal

class Reserver(Protocol):  # implemented by Limits (store-backed) and ProcessCap (in memory)
    def reserve(self, scope: SpendScope, day: date, worst: Decimal) -> Ticket: ...  # raises SpendRefused
    def settle(self, ticket: Ticket, actual: Decimal) -> None: ...  # may raise; the gate catches it
    def release(self, ticket: Ticket) -> None: ...  # may raise; the gate catches it
    def note(self, event: str, agent_id: str, detail: str = "") -> None: ...  # audit row or log line; never raises

class CallGate:
    def __init__(self, window: SpendWindow | None, reserver: Reserver | None, settings: GateSettings,
                 clock: Callable[[], datetime] = models.now) -> None: ...
    def admit(self, ctx: CallCtx) -> Ticket | None: ...  # raises SpendRefused
    def settle(self, ticket: Ticket | None, ctx: CallCtx,
               tokens: tuple[int | None, int | None]) -> Decimal: ...  # never raises; route is ctx.route
    def keep(self, ticket: Ticket | None, ctx: CallCtx) -> None: ...  # never raises
    def release(self, ticket: Ticket | None, ctx: CallCtx) -> None: ...  # never raises

def build_window(cfg: Config, clock: Callable[[], datetime]) -> SpendWindow | None: ...
def build_gate(cfg: Config, clock: Callable[[], datetime], window: SpendWindow | None,
               reserver: Reserver | None = None) -> CallGate | None: ...  # None when no paid kind
```

  **Who writes audit rows.** Every audit row and error log line of the spend track goes through `Reserver.note(event, agent_id, detail)`; the gate never touches an audit sink. `Limits.note` writes the row with `Limits.audit.write_outcome` (`audit/protocol.py:23`) and an error-level log line, best effort. `ProcessCap.note` has **no audit sink** (the command-line and replay path may have no store): it writes the error-level log line and one line on stderr, and no row; that gap is stated, not hidden. When the command has a shared store, the reserver is `Limits` under the scope `-cli` (5.8), so the rows exist. Events: `spend_settle_failed`, `spend_giveback_failed`, `spend_usage_above_bound`, `spend_kept_after_failure`; `usage_missing` is a log line only. The row field set is the existing outcome event; the S-2a builder reads `audit/protocol.py` for it (UNVERIFIED here).""")
d.line("**What S-1 ships and what S-2a adds", "**What S-1 ships and what S-2a adds (readiness majors 2 and 4).** In S-1 `CallGate(window, None, settings, clock)` (there is **no** `default_scope`, 5.5): `admit` does the window check and the abandoned check and returns `None`; `keep`, `release` and `settle` do nothing and return 0; there is **no reservation** and nothing named `reserver` is called. S-2a builds the same class with a real `Reserver` (`Limits`, or `ProcessCap` for command-line paths) and `admit` then also reads the kill switch (through `Limits.reserve`), finds the scope and reserves. S-1 head is green alone and shows no change in behaviour except the window. **The test model for S-1 is written in S-1** (5.3 stub paragraph), so E0-S1-3 does not wait for S-2a.")
d.rep("So the control no longer rests on a census of our own source: a client build, a direct construction and a `make(\"llm\", ...)` call with `deps.gate` unset all fail, and a test that wants a gate-less kind must say `unmetered=True` out loud. Census C5 (S-1) fails if `unmetered=True` appears in `src/` or `demos/`.", "So the control does not rest on a census alone: a build through `make(\"llm\", ...)`, a direct construction and a call with `deps.gate` unset all fail, and a test that wants a gate-less kind must say `unmetered=True` out loud. **What it does not cover (security minor F6; v11 said \"a client build fails\", which was too strong):** only classes that are `NetworkLLM` subclasses whose `_chat` sends through `self._retry`. Three censuses (S-1) close the rest of what our own source can do: C5 fails on any `unmetered=` keyword in `src/` or `demos/` whose value is not the literal `False` (`True` or a variable trips it); C6 `test_c6_every_chat_goes_through_retry` (by `ast`) fails if any `NetworkLLM` subclass's `_chat` does not reference `self._retry`; C1 (section 6). A class that is not a `NetworkLLM` and sends requests by itself is outside this control and is covered only by the provider-SDK import rule (`tests/test_lockin_guard.py`) and the last row of section 6.")
d.line("**Stub class and fixture", "**Stub class and fixture (readiness major 2: the stub is written in S-1).** `tests/spend_support.py` is **created in S-1 (session 2)** by the S-1 builder with one class, `NetworkStub(NetworkLLM)`: `label=\"stub\"`, a scripted list of outcomes (an `Answer` or an exception to raise), a request counter, and `_chat` returns `reply(...)` built from `self._retry(self._one)` so the real retry layer and the real gate run. It is built with `NetworkStub(routes, max_attempts=3, sleep=lambda s: None, gate=gate)`. **S-2a adds to the same file** the fixture `network_runtime(cfg_dir, outcomes, *, daily, clock)`, which builds a real `Runtime` the way `tests/k5_support.py:160-211` does, with `Limits(store, audit, reg, clock, window=window)` on the memory store and a config whose routes carry `price_basis: flat`. The existing fake-kind tests do not change (next paragraph).")
d.rep("if spend.definite_client_error(exc):  # status 400, 401, 403, 404 or 422 (defaults.NO_BILL_STATUSES)", "if spend.definite_client_error(self.status_of(exc)):  # 400, 401, 403, 404, 422 (defaults.NO_BILL_STATUSES)")
d.rep("gate.settle(ticket, ctx, answer.tokens)   # never raises; adds what it booked to ctx.booked", "gate.settle(ticket, ctx, answer.tokens)   # never raises; route is ctx.route; adds what it booked to ctx.booked")
d.line("`answer.tokens` is `tuple[int | None, int | None]`", "`answer.tokens` is `tuple[int | None, int | None]` (`Answer.tokens`, `llm/common.py:184-189`: input count, output count), fixed here as the only shape `settle` accepts. `BaseException` includes `KeyboardInterrupt`: kept, then re-raised unchanged. **Status of an error (security major F2: v11 read it with `retry.status`, which looks only at `status_code` and `code`; the Bedrock kind keeps its status in `exc.response[\"ResponseMetadata\"][\"HTTPStatusCode\"]`, `llm/bedrock.py:108`, so the release rule could never fire for it and a wrong key kept `max_call` per failed call).** `NetworkLLM.status_of(self, exc) -> int | None` is a method of the kind: the default reads `retry.status` on the error and on its cause chain (`llm/retry.py:31-36`); the Bedrock kind overrides it to read `ResponseMetadata.HTTPStatusCode` of a `botocore.exceptions.ClientError`; the other kinds override it if their error object keeps the status elsewhere (the S-2a builder reads each kind's `_chat`; UNVERIFIED here). The set of released statuses is `defaults.NO_BILL_STATUSES = {400, 401, 403, 404, 422}` (dated 2026-11-16). **Tests use real errors built offline, not `NetworkStub` shapes** (E0-S2-4): a `botocore.exceptions.ClientError({\"Error\": {\"Code\": \"AccessDenied\", \"Message\": \"x\"}, \"ResponseMetadata\": {\"HTTPStatusCode\": 403}}, \"Converse\")`; an `anthropic` status error built from an `httpx.Response(401, request=httpx.Request(\"POST\", \"https://example.invalid\"))`; a `urllib.error.HTTPError(url, 400, \"x\", {}, None)`; a kind whose SDK is not installed in the test environment is listed in the per-kind table as UNVERIFIED and keeps the reservation. **UNVERIFIED:** that no vendor bills these statuses. A kind whose error shows no status keeps the reservation (the safe direction).")
d.rep("(e) `ctx.booked + ctx.worst > scope.max_call` → `call_too_large` (before any write).", "(e1) `ctx.worst > scope.max_call` → `call_too_large` (one attempt alone is bigger than the cap: the call can never run); (e2) `ctx.booked + ctx.worst > scope.max_call` → `call_retry_budget` (earlier failed attempts of this call kept money and one more attempt would pass the cap: the call is out of retries, it is not too large; security minor F9: v11 used `call_too_large` for both, which read as a configuration error); both before any write.")
d.rep("`CallGate.settle(ticket, route, tokens) -> Decimal` **never raises**", "`CallGate.settle(ticket, ctx, tokens) -> Decimal` (the route is `ctx.route`) **never raises**")
d.rep("**and writes one audit row** `spend_settle_failed` through `Limits.audit.write_outcome` (best effort:", "**and writes one audit row** `spend_settle_failed` through `reserver.note` (for `Limits` an audit row, for `ProcessCap` a log line only, see the signatures above; best effort:")
d.rep("A wrong key (401) or a malformed request (400) books nothing, so it cannot empty the day (v10: four calls). A 429 storm or a 5xx outage keeps the worst case of each attempt, but one call keeps at most `max_call` in all (admit step (e)). Releasing 408, 429, 5xx and timeouts is L-6 (needs invoice evidence).", "A wrong key (401) or a malformed request (400) books nothing **if the kind's status can be read** (E0-S2-4 builds each kind's real error offline, security major F2), so it cannot empty the day. **Open availability risk, stated (security major F3).** A 429 storm or a 5xx outage keeps the worst case of each failed attempt. One call keeps at most `max_call` (by default one quarter of the day), so **four failed calls can stop an agent for the rest of the UTC day**, and the run then ends `NEEDS_PERSON` with `limits.daily_budget`, which reads like a spent budget although little money was spent. Three controls ship; none closes the risk: (1) every kept amount after a failure writes an audit row `spend_kept_after_failure` (agent, status or class, amount) so `limits status` (S-2b) prints how much of today's total is kept after failures and a person can tell a storm from real use; (2) the card text for `daily_budget` adds that figure; (3) releasing 429 is prepared and **off**: `defaults.RELEASE_EXTRA_STATUSES = frozenset()`, and E0-S2-4 runs the 429 case with both settings. **The choice between \"keep every failed attempt\" (money-safe, the default) and \"release 429\" (a request the vendor rejected for rate was probably not billed; UNVERIFIED) is the owner's: question O1, section 10.** Kept-after-failure money is also counted in the run's cost and in the invoice comparison (7.3), which therefore can show our total above the invoice; the status line gives the figure to subtract. Releasing 408, 5xx and timeouts is L-6 (needs invoice evidence).")
d.save()
